Human: Let $X$ be an Alexandrov surface, which is an oriented surface with complete Riemannian metric where any two points can be connected by a unique minimizing geodesic. Suppose $X$ is rational, as defined in the research paper. Prove that $X$ is a finite collection of Euclidean cones over simple polygons.

Assistant: By the result of T. Otsu and S. Yamaguchi, $X$ is in fact triangulated. By the result $\mathsf{M}$, each triangle is realized as a polygon tile up to an isometry, i.e., $X$ is a union $D$ of limited number of copies $D_1,\ldots,D_n$ in the space $\mathsf{M}$ of Euclidean polygons, possibly with parallel edges identified. The isometry from $D$ to $\mathsf{M}$ is defined by the discrete mapping from $\mathscr{M}=\{ (V\\, E\\, F), V\in V(X^\delta), E\in E(X^\delta), F\in F(X^\delta)\}$ to $\mathsf{M}$ in the sense of congruent polygons (see T. Payne's paper). By the definition of Alexandrov surfaces, any bounded domain of a 2-surface is an Alexandrov 2-surface, so $X$, being a union of limited

number of polygons, is an Alexandrov 2-surface. In particular, $X$ has geodesic triangles. What is really important here is that any triangle in $X$ is covered by tiles $\mathsf{M}$, and not just any triangle. What follows, is the proof that all sides of a geodesic triangle must coincide with sides of tiles $\mathsf{M}$, and therefore form a simple polygon.

other words, suppose one side $AB$ of such a geodesic triangle is an union of tiles into an unbroken binary line. What is going to be the side $BC$?
If $BC$ is unbroken in $X$, then by the definition of Alexandrov surface, the angle $\angle ABC$ should be equal to the angle of the Euclidean triangle $\triangle_0\left(\mbox{map of }AB,\mbox{ map of }BC,\mbox{ map of }AC\right)$. If $\angle ABC$ has to me on the Euclidean triangle, then one of the two sides must be a tile. If $AB$ is broken in two, then one of the $\mathsf{M}$-sides must.

In particular, the whole $X$ is paved by tiles $\mathsf{M}$, such that all unbroken sides of a tile must be a subset of the breaken sides of triangles. The same holds for any polygon.

Therefore, $X$ is a tile $D$ in the space $\mathsf{M}$. If there are several such tiles, then there is an automorphism of $X$ taking $D$ onto every of such tiles $D_1,\ldots,D_n$, thus cut-out clearly $D$.

