Theorem: If $f$ is continuous on the closed interval $[a,\,b]$, then $\int_a^b f(x)\,dx$ is well defined. This meaning that if a function $g(y) = \int_a^b f(y,\,x)\,dx$ can be expressed with $f$ continuous, then $g(y)\equiv C$, where $C$ is some constant, is manifest. Furthermore, if $g(y)$ is not continuous, then there must be some point of discontinuity $a < y_0 < b$... but $g(y)\equiv C$ means that there is no point of discontinuity to identify. Hence, the left-hand side of your theorem is $C$, and so is the right-hand side, and so the theorem is valid: $f$ continuous implies $C$.
Now, your function $g$ may not necessarily be continuous. Picky. Suppose a part of the graph of $f$ has a jump discontinuity, meaning at that point $y_0$ the colored part of the graph has a discontinuity in red. Call the area that is colored above that very area $C$. For all $y$ not in the interval $[y_0+n-1,y_0+n)$ for $n$ a positive integer, this area will be 0. However, the integral will include the entire area above the red joey! The value of the integral on the interval is clearly $0 \cdot \infty$, which is undefined! And so we have found a point of discontinuity in $g$, which is what I was counting.
Summary: $f$ continuous implies $g$ continuous.  $f$ bounded Lipschitz continuous implies $g$ Lipschitz-continuous, and in the sense of measures implies $g$.  And of course, it follows trivially from general measures theory.

Why is the uniform probability that an integer drawn from [0, 10) is equal to 10 greater than 0, but equal to 0 the uniform probability that an integer drawn from the same interval is a number less than 10? The uniform probability that an integer drawn from [0, 10) is equal to 10 is greater than 0 because the interval [0, 10) is a continuous distribution. In a continuous distribution, any two distinct values are infinitely close to one another, and so the probability that a randomly chosen value is equal to any particular value is greater than zero.

The uniform probability that an integer drawn from the same interval is a number less than 10, however, is equal to 0 because the interval [0, 10) is not a continuous distribution. In a discrete distribution, any two distinct values are distinct, and so the probability that a randomly chosen value is equal to any particular value is zero.

